# Documentation discipline

The docs are the system's long-term memory. Future agents will not see this conversation. Anything not written down is lost, and a business rule that exists only in code is a defect.

## Protocol (same commit as the code, no exceptions)

| Trigger | Required update |
|---|---|
| Add, move, rename, or delete a file or directory | `docs/file-map.md`: add/move/remove the entry with a one-line purpose. Moves list the new path only; old paths go in the changelog. |
| Add or change behavior, a rule, a limit, a state transition, a default, or an edge case | The matching `docs/business-logic/<domain>.md`: add or edit the rule with its ID, invariant, and edge cases. |
| Any behavioral, structural, or user-visible change | `CHANGELOG.md` under `## [Unreleased]` (Added / Changed / Removed / Fixed / Security), citing rule IDs and ADRs. |
| New or changed architecture, stack, schema-shape, or cross-service decision | New ADR in `docs/adr/` from `docs/adr/template.md`; update `docs/decisions.md` and the ADR index. Supersede old ADRs; never rewrite their decision. |
| New domain term | `docs/glossary.md`. |
| New service, or a change to how a service is built, run, configured, or tested | That service's `AGENTS.md` (template: `docs/architecture/service-agents.md`). |
| New or changed run/test command | The "How to run and test" section of `AGENTS.md` and the service `AGENTS.md`. |
| New cross-service data flow | `docs/architecture/overview.md`. |

## Business rules

- Every rule has a stable ID in its domain doc: `OE-` orders and execution, `VP-` virtual portfolios and attribution, `RM-` risk management, `SM-` strategies and model lifecycle, `DF-` data and features, `OP-` operations (build, run, deploy, backup). IDs are never reused; retired rules are marked `Retired` with the reason.
- Code that enforces a rule cites the ID in a short comment at the enforcement point. Tests that verify it include the ID in the test name or description.
- If code and docs disagree, stop: determine the intended behavior, then fix both in one commit. Never weaken a safety rule in the docs to match code; escalate instead.
- Write rules as testable statements (MUST / MUST NOT, with the exact limit or condition), not prose descriptions of the code.

## Style

- Keep docs short and current; edit in place rather than appending history (history lives in git and the changelog).
- Mark unbuilt items `Planned` and undecided items `DECISION PENDING` rather than omitting them.
- Link, don't duplicate: one fact lives in one place.

## Done checklist

Before you commit, confirm each line or state why it does not apply:

- [ ] `docs/file-map.md` matches every file/dir I added, moved, or removed.
- [ ] Every behavior I added or changed is described in `docs/business-logic/` with a rule ID, invariant, and edge cases.
- [ ] Enforcement code and tests cite the rule IDs.
- [ ] `CHANGELOG.md` has an `[Unreleased]` entry.
- [ ] Any new decision has an ADR, and `docs/decisions.md` is updated.
- [ ] New terms are in `docs/glossary.md`.
- [ ] The relevant `AGENTS.md` files (root and service) are still accurate, including run/test commands.
- [ ] No secrets, credentials, account numbers, or real P&L appear in docs, examples, or fixtures.
